// An ordinary variable may share a module alias's name. `alias::fn(…)` and `alias.CONST`
// address the module namespace, which is separate from the variable environment, so they
// must keep resolving to the module regardless of what the variable holds.
//
// The tree-walker has always kept import aliases in their own table. The web interpreter
// (web/zymbol.js) parsed `::` and `.` into the same node and resolved the object as an
// ordinary expression, so once a variable took the alias's name the module became
// unreachable and the call failed with "'.<name>' requires a named tuple" — an error
// naming the wrong operator and the wrong problem.
//
// This is not a contrived pattern: zyKlingonGalaxy imports `Duj` (ship) under a Klingon
// alias and then uses that same name for the player's ship value, so every left/right move
// runs `duj = duj::bIj(duj, …)`. The game was unplayable in the browser because of it.

<# ./hlz/salida => s

// The alias resolves to the module before anything shadows it.
>> "const antes:   " (s.LIMITE) ¶

// Now let a plain variable take the same name.
s = 10
>> "variable:      " s ¶

// Both module accesses must still work, and the variable must be unaffected by them.
>> "const despues: " (s.LIMITE) ¶
>> "slice:         " (s::cola("abcdef", 2)) ¶
>> "variable otra vez: " s ¶

// Reassigning through a call whose callee is the shadowed alias — the exact shape the
// Klingon game uses on every move.
s = s::cola("XY9", 1)
>> "reasignada:    " s ¶
